    "answer": "Episode\u202f4 explains Shannon\u2019s source\u2011coding theorem as the statement that **the entropy of a source is the exact, unbeatable lower bound on how much you can compress its messages without losing information**. In other words, you can design coding schemes that squeeze a message down to its entropy and get arbitrarily close to that limit, but you can never push below it (unless you\u2019re willing to discard information)\u202f\u2014\u202fthe \u201cfloor\u201d of compression\u202f\u3010Episode\u202f4, 14:30\u201115:59\u3011.  \n\nThe podcast then illustrates the idea with everyday examples: a text file zips dramatically because language is highly redundant, while a file that\u2019s already compressed or pure random noise (already near its entropy) barely shrinks at all\u202f\u3010Episode\u202f4, 16:00\u201117:24\u3011.  \n\nSo, according to episode\u202f4, Shannon\u2019s source\u2011coding theorem tells us that **entropy measures the true amount of \u201csurprise\u201d per symbol, and lossless compression can approach\u2014but never beat\u2014that amount**.",
